From: GameShellNeo contributors
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: balance SDIO IRQ runtime references on state changes

The threaded SDIO core wakes its interrupt thread on retained-power resume.
That thread rearms the IRQ even when it was already enabled. Taking a new
runtime reference on every enable leaks one per such resume. Repeated disable
calls can also consume a reference owned by another runtime-PM user.

Use the existing cached SDIO mask to change reference ownership only on an
actual enable/disable transition. Serialize the reference accounting and
cached mask with the existing IRQ lock. The non-sleeping get precedes register
access, and the non-sleeping put follows masking. Continue to program IMASK on
repeated requests, including after controller reset, preserving unrelated bits.

No autosuspend, card-power or brcmfmac policy is changed.

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -975,12 +975,15 @@
 {
 	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
 	unsigned long flags;
+	bool was_enabled;
 	u32 imask;
 
-	if (enable)
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, flags);
+
+	/* Resume can rearm an already enabled IRQ; own one reference per state. */
+	was_enabled = !!host->sdio_imask;
+	if (enable && !was_enabled)
 		pm_runtime_get_noresume(host->dev);
-
-	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, flags);
 
 	imask = mmc_readl(host, REG_IMASK);
 	if (enable) {
@@ -991,10 +994,12 @@
 		imask &= ~SDXC_SDIO_INTERRUPT;
 	}
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_IMASK, imask);
+
+	/* The non-sleeping put follows the hardware mask, under the same lock. */
+	if (!enable && was_enabled)
+		pm_runtime_put_noidle(host->dev);
+
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, flags);
-
-	if (!enable)
-		pm_runtime_put_noidle(host->mmc->parent);
 }
 
 static void sunxi_mmc_hw_reset(struct mmc_host *mmc)
